{% extends '_article.jinja' %}
{% import 'components.jinja' as ui with context %}
{% import '_code.jinja' as code with context %}
{% from 'data/pages.jinja' import catalogue %}
{% set article = catalogue['getting-started'] %}
{% set crumbs = [] %}
{% set plate_key = none %}
{% block article_body %}
<p class="wf-lede">The program on this page is a test in the site&rsquo;s sample crate. It starts a real server on a loopback port, connects a real client, sends a <code>Ping</code>, and checks the <code>Pong</code>. Every panel below is cut from that file, and the crate runs against wireframe {{ wireframe_version }} with Rust {{ rust_verified }}.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="dependencies"><h2 id="dependencies">Add the dependencies</h2>
<p>wireframe runs on Tokio and, by default, encodes messages with bincode 2. The sample crate also uses <code>async-trait</code> to write middleware, and turns on two opt-in features: <code>pool</code> for client connection pools and <code>testkit</code> for the test drivers. Neither is needed for a first server.</p>
{{ code.code('Cargo.toml', 'deps', title='Cargo.toml', lexer='toml') }}
{% call ui.callout('warning', 'Pin tokio-util before the first build') %}<p>wireframe {{ wireframe_version }} fails to compile against tokio-util 0.7.20, which added a second <code>Encoder</code> implementation for <code>LengthDelimitedCodec</code> and made a conversion inside wireframe ambiguous (error E0283). A fresh dependency resolves 0.7.20. Pin {{ tokio_util_pin }} in the lockfile until a fixed release of wireframe ships:</p>{% endcall %}
{% call code.command('Pin tokio-util') %}cargo update -p tokio-util --precise {{ tokio_util_pin }}{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="messages"><h2 id="messages">Describe the messages</h2>
<p>A message is any type bincode can encode and decode. Deriving <code>bincode::Encode</code> and <code>bincode::BorrowDecode</code> is enough: wireframe implements its <code>Message</code> trait for such types, which provides <code>to_bytes</code> and <code>from_bytes</code>. The message ID is not part of the type. It travels in the envelope, so the same type could be routed under any ID.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'messages', title='first_server.rs · messages') }}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="answer"><h2 id="answer">Route and answer</h2>
<p>A route maps a message ID to a handler. In {{ wireframe_version }} a handler is an <code>Arc</code>&rsquo;d closure that receives a reference to the envelope and returns a boxed future of <code>()</code>. It can inspect the request, log it, or hand work elsewhere, but it does not return the reply.</p>
<p>Replies are written in middleware. Each route&rsquo;s handler is wrapped in a <code>HandlerService</code>, and the default service answers with the request&rsquo;s own payload, so an unwrapped route is an echo. A middleware layer calls the inner service, then replaces the reply&rsquo;s bytes.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'middleware', title='first_server.rs · the reply') }}
<p>With the middleware in place, the application is a route and a wrap. <code>WireframeApp::new()</code> is fallible, as is each builder step, so the whole chain returns <code>wireframe::Result</code>.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'app', title='first_server.rs · the application') }}
{% call ui.callout('fact', 'middleware::from_fn does not fit WireframeApp::wrap') %}<p>The upstream user&rsquo;s guide builds this middleware with <code>middleware::from_fn</code>. In {{ wireframe_version }} that does not compile: <code>wrap</code> accepts only a <code>Transform</code> whose output is a <code>HandlerService</code>, and <code>from_fn</code> produces a different service type. The <code>Transform</code> above is the pattern the upstream <code>ping_pong</code> example uses.</p>{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="serve"><h2 id="serve">Serve it</h2>
<p><code>WireframeServer::new</code> takes a factory rather than an application: in {{ wireframe_version }} it calls the factory once for every accepted connection, so each connection starts from a freshly built application. Binding moves the server into its bound state; port 0 asks the operating system for a free port, and <code>local_addr</code> reports which one it chose. <code>ready_signal</code> fires once the workers are accepting, and <code>run_with_shutdown</code> stops them when its future completes.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'serve', title='first_server.rs · the server') }}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="call"><h2 id="call">Call it</h2>
<p>The client shares the server&rsquo;s defaults: the same length-delimited framing, the same bincode serializer, the same envelope. <code>call_correlated</code> stamps a fresh correlation ID on the request, sends it, reads the reply, and fails with <code>ClientError::CorrelationMismatch</code> if the reply carries a different one.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/first_server.rs', 'call', title='first_server.rs · the client') }}
<p>Run the program with the rest of the sample crate:</p>
{% call code.command('Run the samples') %}cargo test --test first_server{% endcall %}
</section>

